  63: ## 2026-11-15 — Real-Time Fluid Scroll Progress Tracking in Navbar
  64: 
  65: ### Objective
  66: Resolve issue where the navbar glowing progress bar only updated when scrolling stopped instead of tracking smoothly in real time during continuous scrolling.
  67: 
  68: ### Root Cause Analysis
  69: 1. **React State Batching Delay**: Scroll events triggered `setScrollProgress(progress)`. In React 19 concurrent mode during active scroll events, React throttles/defers state re-renders to prevent jank, causing the state to flush only when scroll momentum stops.
  70: 2. **CSS Transition Stacking**: `transition-[width] duration-75 ease-out` caused the progress bar to fight CSS interpolation on every frame instead of updating synchronously.
  71: 
  72: ### Changes Made
  73: - **Direct DOM Ref Manipulation**: Bound a direct element reference (`progressBarRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)`) to the inner progress bar. Removed `const [scrollProgress, setScrollProgress] = useState(0)`.
  74: - **Physical RAF Lerp Damping**:
  75:   - Implemented `requestAnimationFrame` interpolation (`currentProgress += (target - current) * 0.35`).
  76:   - Responds on the very first sub-pixel scroll tick and updates smoothly at 120fps/60fps.
  77:   - Automatically settles with zero redundant animation frame consumption when idle (`isTicking = false`).
  78: - **Eliminated CSS Transition Latency**: Removed `transition-[width] duration-75 ease-out` so the bar tracks real-time scroll position without delay.
  79: - **Optimized `setHasScrolled`**: Only triggers state updates when crossing the 24px threshold (`prev !== scrolled ? scrolled : prev`), completely eliminating component re-renders while scrolling.
